Ruling: the native surfaces live in `src/native` and publish as `./native`. Its package export takes the import-only shape `./browser` and `./vue` use (package.json:42-53). A `srcNative` factory beside `srcVue` (vite.config.ts:248-280) builds it. A wrapper `configs/src/vite.native.config.ts` externalizes `@orkestrel/veneer` and `@orkestrel/veneer/browser` exactly as configs/src/vite.vue.config.ts:10-34 does.

Externalizing matters because three pieces of per-document state live in module statics, and each must stay one instance shared by both entries:
- the lock's (src/browser/Lock.ts:13);
- the registry's (src/browser/Registry.ts:16);
- the scope map (src/browser/Veneer.ts:21).

A wrapper over `srcBrowser` cannot do this job. `mergeOverride` (vite.config.ts:85-92) concatenates the plugin arrays, so `environmentBoundary('src/browser')` (vite.config.ts:209) would sit beside the native boundary. The factory belongs in the scaffold-generated root config.

Names:
- the face is `native`;
- the entity is `Dialog`, one word that names no Bootstrap entity;
- `DialogInterface`, `DialogOptions`, `DialogEventMap`, and `DIALOG_EVENTS` go in src/native/types.ts and constants.ts;
- `createDialogPlugin` goes in src/native/plugins.ts, `createDialog` in factories.ts, and `isBrowserDialog` in validators.ts;
- `DIALOG_OPEN` and `DIALOG_HOST` join `VeneerErrorCode` (src/core/types.ts:55-62);
- later native entities take their own words: `Float` for a top-layer panel, and a disclosure entity.

The drop-in engine keeps these byte for byte against the stage B base:
- the entity and mechanism files: Alert, Button, Carousel, Dropdown, Modal, Offcanvas, Scrollspy, Tab, Toast, Tip, Placement, Backdrop, Trap, Hold, Swipe, Registry, and Veneer;
- Collapse.ts and plugins.ts, restored by unit N0a to their bytes at 6b99552^;
- the kind files helpers.ts (including `emitEvent` at :787-812), constants.ts, parsers.ts, validators.ts, factories.ts, and index.ts.

Two files change:
- Lock.ts gains one optional parameter.
- types.ts gains the `LockInterface.acquire(width?)` signature and its `width` TSDoc, and loses the B4 declarations.

`createBootstrapPlugins()` (src/browser/plugins.ts:382-397) stays the complete drop-in collection.

Law: scaffold AGENTS.md:27-28 names `vue` as the one browser extension and reads `src/<name>` as a styles extension. workspace.md:25-26 defines `src/<name>` as a sheet face. scaffold AGENTS.md:26 limits app/browser imports to src/core and src/browser. ROADMAP.md:3 forbids inventing a layout. `./native` therefore needs a scaffold amendment and the user's ruling R1. The amendment covers:
- a named TypeScript browser extension over src/browser, admitted by the law;
- its alias;
- its Oxlint block, in the pattern of .oxlintrc.json:182-190 and :398-405;
- a `src:native` project;
- app/browser importing it, for the showcase.

Fallback if R1 declines: `Dialog.ts` and the native kind declarations land as root files of src/browser, exported through `./browser`. That fallback has three costs:
- the boundary moves from lint and bundles to tests;
- the drop-in bundle carries native code;
- the consumer reads the contract from names, not from the import path.

A subfolder `src/browser/native/` is refused, because entity subfolders are lowercase plural class folders that never grow centralized files (architecture.md:228-230).

Reasons for `./native` over in-browser files:
- The import path tells the consumer which contract it loads.
- `dist/src/browser/index.js` carries no native class, so the distribution proof can refuse native trigger APIs in the drop-in bundle.
- Lint and the Vite environment boundary enforce the import direction.
- The native layer can reach the engine only through its public barrel (src/browser/index.ts:1-27). Every engine change it needs is therefore a reviewed public seam.

classes:
Ruling: separate classes, not leaves. `Dialog` implements `DialogInterface`. Its members mirror `ModalInterface` (src/browser/types.ts:618-639): `visible`, `phase`, `show(related?)`, `hide()`, `toggle(related?)`, and `update()`, plus `element`, `destroyed`, and `destroy` (src/browser/types.ts:451-458).

The host must be an `HTMLDialogElement` in its own realm, checked by `isBrowserDialog`. Any other host throws `DIALOG_HOST`.

`Dialog` carries the verdict's measured native mechanics (verdict:451-507):
- `closedby="none"` held through `Hold` while shown;
- `showModal()` at the display write and `close()` after the hide transition;
- the native marker, which never spans a queued event;
- host listeners for `command`, `cancel`, and `beforetoggle`, routed through `show` and `hide`;
- forced close by phase, and rollback of a cancelled or thrown open;
- `DIALOG_OPEN`, in place of `MODAL_OPEN`;
- Escape on keydown, and the backdrop on mousedown plus click.

Neither a Bootstrap path nor markup config sits inside it:
- It reads no `data-bs-*` attribute.
- `DialogOptions` holds `dismiss`, reusing `OverlayDismissOptions` (src/browser/types.ts:862-875), and `on`. It has no `focus` key and no `native` or `stable` leaf.

Shared through the public barrel, unchanged:
- `Veneer` routing (src/browser/Veneer.ts:66-73, :177-206) and `buildPlugin` (src/browser/helpers.ts:68-149);
- `Registry` and `ComponentContext`;
- `Hold`, for the `closedby` slot;
- `Trap`, the sole focus wrapper, per verdict:810-812;
- `Backdrop`, with its default `.modal-backdrop` node (src/browser/Backdrop.ts:43-47);
- `awaitTransition` (src/browser/helpers.ts:917-954), `reflow`, `bindEventMap`, `restoreFocus`, and `isBrowserElement`;
- `VisibilityPhase` and `OverlayDismissOptions`;
- `Placement`, for later floats.

Shared with one seam: `Lock.acquire(width?: number)`.
- A first acquisition uses the argument in place of the measurement at src/browser/Lock.ts:59, and a joining acquisition (:46-57) ignores it.
- Modal and Offcanvas call `acquire()` with no argument (src/browser/Modal.ts:121, src/browser/Offcanvas.ts:123), so the default `width ?? measured` reproduces stage A.
- `Dialog` passes `0` when `getComputedStyle(documentElement).scrollbarGutter` begins with `stable`. That read lives in src/native/helpers.ts, not in Lock.ts.
- `LockInterface.width` (src/browser/types.ts:2243) is re-documented as the first acquisition's compensation while locked. It equals the measurement on every page whose overlays pass nothing.
- This replaces the verdict's `LockOptions`, `compensation`, and `reserved` (verdict:564-596) and the `Modal.update` edit (verdict:604), so Modal.ts stays untouched.

Not shared:
- `emitEvent`, which hard-codes `cancelable: true` (src/browser/helpers.ts:800). Native events go through `emitNotice(element, name, detail, cancelable)` in src/native/helpers.ts.
- `MODAL_EVENTS`, `Modal`, and `Tip` internals.

Duplicated: `Dialog` repeats Modal's show, hide, veto, backdrop, static-bounce, and update orchestration (src/browser/Modal.ts:116-257), because every line there runs on the Bootstrap path. The parity projection test pins the shared footprint to Bootstrap's oracle, so drift fails a test.

One cross-layer duty moves with it. A Bootstrap tip hides on its closest `.modal`'s `hide.bs.modal` (src/browser/Tip.ts:96-98), and a native host carries no `.modal`. At every accepted or forced hide, `Dialog` therefore hides each shown tip whose trigger lies inside it:
- candidates come from `[aria-describedby]`, which a shown tip writes (src/browser/Tip.ts:202);
- `Registry.component` looks each candidate up with `createTooltipPlugin()` and `createPopoverPlugin()` as keys (src/browser/Registry.ts:35-47);
- `Dialog` then calls `TipInterface.hide`.

How the hot paths stay unbranched:
- No Bootstrap module imports a native module, enforced by lint under R1 and by an import test.
- `Dialog` neither subclasses nor wraps `Modal`.
- The single shared hot-path edit is the `??` default in `Lock.acquire`.

Scaffold AGENTS.md:65 prefers one interface and one shared engine. The user's ruling of 2026-10-06 (rulings:18) overrides that preference here. `DialogInterface` keeps `ModalInterface`'s member names, so a consumer learns one shape.
